Let's understand Condition Variables in C++ with a simple example.

A condition variable is used when one thread needs to wait until another thread performs some operation.

Unlike a mutex, it avoids busy waiting.
1. Real-life example
Imagine two threads:
- Thread 1 (Consumer): Wants to consume data but the data is not available.
- Thread 2 (Producer): Produces the data.
Without a condition variable, Thread 1 might continuously check whether data is available.

With a condition variable:
1. Thread 1 acquires the lock.
2. Checks whether data is available.
3. If not, it goes to sleep and releases the lock.
4. Thread 2 produces data and notifies Thread 1.
5. Thread 1 wakes up, reacquires the lock and consumes the dat
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
using namespace std;

mutex m;
condition_variable cv;

bool ready = false;

void consumer() {

    unique_lock<mutex> lock(m);

    cout << "Consumer is waiting...\n";

    cv.wait(lock, [] {
        return ready;
    });

    cout << "Consumer received the signal!\n";
    cout << "Processing data...\n";
}

void producer() {

    this_thread::sleep_for(chrono::seconds(2));

    {
        lock_guard<mutex> lock(m);

        ready = true;

        cout << "Producer produced data\n";
    }

    cv.notify_one();
}

int main() {

    thread t1(consumer);
    thread t2(producer);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();

    return 0;
}
